# Documentation Rules

Documentation should capture durable knowledge.

Do not document every line of code.

Document important:

- architecture decisions;
- business rules;
- API contracts;
- deployment configuration;
- security decisions;
- important bugs;
- lessons learned;
- major milestones.

## OBSIDIAN

Obsidian is the project's human knowledge base.

Use it for:

"What did we decide?"

"Why did we decide it?"

"What did I learn?"

"How did we solve this?"

"How do I reproduce this setup?"

Do not store:

- passwords;
- JWT secrets;
- API keys;
- .env values;
- private credentials.

When a change creates durable knowledge, suggest an Obsidian note update.

The code remains the source of truth for implementation.